    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Blood pressure is measured using two numbers: systolic (top number) and diastolic (bottom number), recorded in millimeters of mercury (mm Hg). According to guidelines from the American Heart Association , blood pressure categories are defined as follows:",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "American Heart Association",
            "link": "https://www.heart.org/en/health-topics/high-blood-pressure/understanding-blood-pressure-readings"
          }
        ],
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "systolic (top number) and diastolic (bottom number)"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Normal: Less than 120 / less than 80 mm Hg"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Elevated: 120\u2013129 / less than 80 mm Hg"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Stage 1 High Blood Pressure (Hypertension): 130\u2013139 / 80\u201389 mm Hg"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Stage 2 High Blood Pressure (Hypertension): 140 or higher / 90 or higher mm Hg"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Hypertensive Crisis (Medical Emergency): Higher than 180 / higher than 120 mm Hg",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              2
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "What the Numbers Mean"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Systolic (Top Number): Measures the pressure in your arteries when your heart contracts."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Diastolic (Bottom Number): Measures the pressure in your arteries when your heart rests between beats."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "High Diagnosis: High blood pressure is diagnosed if either the top or bottom number consistently falls into an elevated or hypertensive range .",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "What to Do in a Crisis"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If your blood pressure reading exceeds 180/120 mm Hg, wait five minutes and retest. If your numbers are still that high and you have warning signs like chest pain, shortness of breath, back pain, or trouble speaking, call 911 immediately.",
        "reference_indexes": [
          2,
          3,
          4
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like, let me know:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "The specific blood pressure reading you are wondering about"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Whether you are asking for yourself or someone else"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "If you have any symptoms or existing heart conditions"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can help explain what those numbers mean for your health."
      }
    ],
